# Step 15 — Mail relay (postfix → Brevo)

## Why
Foundation for server-side alerts. Without working outbound mail,
unattended-upgrades / certbot / cron scripts / WordPress contact forms
all fail silently.

## Provider
Brevo (formerly Sendinblue) — free tier 300 emails/day, plenty for alerts.

## 1. Sign up at Brevo
- In dashboard → SMTP & API → SMTP tab
- Note the credentials they provide:
  - SMTP server: smtp-relay.brevo.com
  - Port: 587
  - Login: your Brevo login email
  - SMTP key (master password)

## 2. Install postfix and dependencies

```bash
sudo apt install postfix mailutils libsasl2-modules
```

During the installer:
- "General type of mail configuration" → **Internet Site with smarthost**
- "System mail name" → use the VPS hostname or main site domain
- "SMTP relay host" → **leave blank** (we configure it below)


# Step 16 — Certbot renewal alerts
## 3. Create the credentials file

```bash
sudo nano /etc/postfix/sasl_passwd
```

One line:
[smtp-relay.brevo.com]:587    <brevo-login>:<brevo-smtp-key>

Replace `<brevo-login>` and `<brevo-smtp-key>` with your real values.

Brackets around the hostname matter. Separator between host and user is
whitespace. Separator between user and password is a single colon.

## 4. Lock down permissions and compile to hash format

```bash
sudo chmod 600 /etc/postfix/sasl_passwd
sudo postmap /etc/postfix/sasl_passwd
sudo chmod 600 /etc/postfix/sasl_passwd.db
sudo ls -la /etc/postfix/sasl_passwd*
```

Expected: two files, both `-rw-------` owned by `root:root`.

## 5. Configure postfix

```bash
sudo postconf -e "relayhost = [smtp-relay.brevo.com]:587"
sudo postconf -e "smtp_sasl_auth_enable = yes"
sudo postconf -e "smtp_sasl_password_maps = hash:/etc/postfix/sasl_passwd"
sudo postconf -e "smtp_sasl_security_options = noanonymous"
sudo postconf -e "smtp_tls_security_level = encrypt"
sudo postconf -e "smtp_sasl_tls_security_options = noanonymous"
sudo systemctl reload postfix
```

Verify:

```bash
sudo postconf relayhost smtp_sasl_auth_enable smtp_sasl_password_maps smtp_tls_security_level
```

## 6. Send a test email

```bash
echo "Test from $(hostname) at $(date)" | mail -s "Mail relay test" you@example.com
mailq
sudo journalctl -u postfix --since "1 minute ago" --no-pager
```

Expected:
- `mailq` returns "Mail queue is empty"
- journalctl shows `status=sent (250 ... OK ...)`
- The email arrives in your inbox (check spam)

## Lessons
- "No worthy mechs found" → install `libsasl2-modules`
- OVH's `smtp.vps.ovh.net` relay is deprecated — DNS resolution fails
- Once mail works, WordPress's queued contact-form emails start flowing
  too — set this up before debugging "WP doesn't send mail" issues
- Don't paste SMTP credentials in screenshots/repos; they go in
  /etc/postfix/sasl_passwd with mode 600


# Certbot renewal alerts

Edit the certbot config:

```bash
sudo nano /etc/letsencrypt/cli.ini
```

Add these two lines at the end:
email = haythem@nouslagence.com
no-eff-email = True

## Behavior
- Certbot emails ONLY on errors (default — no success spam)
- Let's Encrypt independently emails the same address when a cert is
  20 / 10 / 7 days from expiring without renewal (defense in depth)

# Unattended-upgrades alerts

```bash
sudo nano /etc/apt/apt.conf.d/50unattended-upgrades
```

Uncomment and edit:

Unattended-Upgrade::Mail "haythem@nouslagence.com";
Unattended-Upgrade::MailReport "only-on-error";

# Daily disk-space check

## Create the script

```bash
sudo nano /etc/cron.daily/disk-check
```

Content:

```bash
#!/bin/sh
THRESHOLD=85
EMAIL=haythem@nouslagence.com

USAGE=$(df / | awk 'NR==2 {print $5}' | tr -d '%')

if [ "$USAGE" -gt "$THRESHOLD" ]; then
    df -h | mail -s "Disk usage at ${USAGE}% on $(hostname)" "$EMAIL"
fi
```
## Make it executable

```bash
sudo chmod +x /etc/cron.daily/disk-check
```

## Verify

```bash
ls -la /etc/cron.daily/disk-check
sudo /etc/cron.daily/disk-check
```

## Test the alert path

Temporarily lower threshold to force an email:

```bash
sudo sed -i 's/^THRESHOLD=85/THRESHOLD=1/' /etc/cron.daily/disk-check
sudo /etc/cron.daily/disk-check
sudo sed -i 's/^THRESHOLD=1/THRESHOLD=85/' /etc/cron.daily/disk-check
```

You'll receive an email subject "Disk usage at XX% on <hostname>".
Confirm receipt, then verify threshold is back to 85.

## Behavior
- Runs once daily via `/etc/cron.daily/` (no separate cron entry needed)
- Below 85%: silent
- Above 85%: emails full `df -h` output